conditions.baseline:
Convert the user's natural-language scenario request into a single strict-JSON intent object that drives an OSM-to-CommonRoad scenario generator. The output JSON must follow exactly this schema:

{
  "location": {
    "city": <string or null>,
    "country_code": <ISO 3166-1 alpha-3 string or null>,
    "bbox": <[south, west, north, east] floats or null>
  },
  "road_classes": <array of strings from {"roads", "link", "all"}>,
  "scenario_type": <string from {"highway", "urban", "intersection", "roundabout", "rural", "ramp", "any"}>,
  "sim": {
    "duration_s": <number, default 20>,
    "density": <"low" | "medium" | "high", default "medium">,
    "vehicle_types": <array of strings from {"car", "truck", "bus", "motorcycle", "bicycle", "trailer", "delivery", "coach", "taxi", "emergency", "moped"}, default ["car"]>
  },
  "ego": {
    "strategy": <"first" | "by_type" | "by_index">,
    "vehicle_type": <string from vehicle_types list, only used when strategy = "by_type">,
    "index": <integer >= 0, only used when strategy = "by_index">
  },
  "goal": {
    "strategy": <"trajectory_offset" | "specific_lanelet">,
    "offset_steps": <integer, only used when strategy = "trajectory_offset", default 50>,
    "lanelet_id": <integer, only used when strategy = "specific_lanelet">,
    "position_keyword": <"start" | "middle" | "end", default "middle">,
    "rectangle_length_m": <number, default 6.0>,
    "rectangle_width_m": <number, default 2.0>
  }
}

Every top-level key MUST be present. Use null (not missing) for nested fields that the user did not specify. Apply the defaults documented in the schema for unspecified fields (density, duration_s, position_keyword, rectangle dimensions). Return ONLY the JSON object, no commentary, no markdown fences.

Extract a location from the user's description. Return your answer in the following JSON format:

{
  "country_code": XXX,
  "specifier": string
}

country_code is the three-letter country code and "specifier" is additional information about the specific city or road. If a field cannot be determined from the user input, exclude it from the output. If neither field can be determined, return an empty JSON object {}.

conditions.cp_icl:
Extract a location from the user's description. Return your answer in the following JSON format:

{
  "country_code": XXX,
  "specifier": string
}

country_code is the three-letter country code and "specifier" is additional information about the specific city or road. If a field cannot be determined from the user input, exclude it from the output. If neither field can be determined, return an empty JSON object {}.

The location includes a three-letter country code as well as a more specific descriptor, which is for rural scenarios a two/three letter city code (e.g. Muc) and for highways/major roads the road code (e.g. A9 or Lanker). The user may not provide you the country code explicitly or only provide a city name, so you may have to use standard ISO 3166-1 alpha-3 country codes. If unsure, use your knowledge of geography.

ZAM: This is a special country code used for artificial road networks that were designed by hand. If a user requests such a road network, return ZAM as the country code.

The second part of the location has to be specified explicitly by the user, i.e. a city or road code must be supplied. Otherwise, exclude that field. Also, if a user only specifies one of the fields, exclude the other one. If neither fields are specified, exclude them both. Only ever return one country_code value, never more than that.

User input: The scenario takes place in Munich in Germany; the city code is Muc.
Output:
{
  "country_code": "DEU",
  "specifier": "Muc"
}
Reasoning: The user specified Germany as a country, for which the code is DEU. He gave Muc as a clearly defined city code, so that fills up the specifier field.

User input: In Belgium. The city is Geneva.
Output:
{
  "country_code": "BEL"
}
Reasoning: The user specified Belgium as a country, but only gave the name of a city, not an actual code. This cannot be processed and therefore we remove the specifier field.

User input: The location does not matter to me.
Output:
{
}
Reasoning: The user gave us no information, so we exclude both fields and return an empty JSON object.

User input: The network should be artificial.
Output:
{
  "country_code": "ZAM"
}
Reasoning: The user did not specify a city/road code, so we exclude that field. However, he has specified that the network should be artificial, which is why we use "ZAM" as a country code.

conditions.baseline:
Extract the initial velocity of the ego car from the user's description. Return the result in one of the following JSON formats:

For an exact value:
{
  "initial_velocity": [float]
}

For a range:
{
  "initial_velocity": [float_lower, float_higher]
}

float_lower describes the lower bound (inclusive) of the velocity interval, whereas float_higher specifies the upper bound (inclusive). If the user does not provide a numerical value or range, return an empty JSON object {}.

